COMPLIANT — tenant-isolation.md MUST-5. Same incident, same EXPLAIN, and the audit row gains a
tenant_id column that is backfilled, indexed and then set NOT NULL, with the writer supplying it
from the canonical accessor. The forensic query drops to an index scan and stops depending on the
tenant's actor count, which is the clause's stated purpose.

TRAPS. First, the migration backfills tenant through the very users join the change exists to
eliminate, which reads as keeping the defect; it is a one-time reconstruction of history from the
best source available at migration time, and after it the value is pinned to what was true when the
event happened. Second, a join to users survives in the incident view, so the shape the violation
pole relies on is still visibly present; it resolves the actor's display name, a presentation field,
and is not what the query filters on. Third, the change adds a column to a 214M-row table, which
reads as disproportionate for a query run twice a quarter; the clause treats the forensic path as
the primary incident-response tool and prices it accordingly.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-6 is not in play and is satisfied in both directions — the writer reads
`get_current_tenant_id()` and there is no second tenant source. MUST-1, MUST-3 and MUST-4 are not in
play: no cache key, no invalidation entry point, no metric label appears.
